---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'why-hex-matters', text: 'Why Hex Forensics?' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'layout-anatomy', text: 'Anatomy of the Hex Inspector' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'event-headers', text: 'Decoding 19-Byte Binlog Event Headers' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Hex Forensic Inspector"
  description="Inspect raw binary payloads, 19-byte event headers, and ASCII byte maps."
  currentSlug="hex-inspector"
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    When diagnosing low-level binlog corruption, replication decode mismatches, or custom protocol adapters, seeing decoded text is not enough. You need to inspect the raw binary byte stream.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-hex-matters">Why Hex Forensics?</h2>
  <p>
    Standard CLI utilities like <code>hexdump</code> or <code>xxd</code> display an entire file indiscriminately. Finding where event #4194381 starts in a 1 GB file requires tedious byte-offset calculation. Binsight's Hex Inspector clips and isolates the exact byte range for the selected event automatically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layout-anatomy">Anatomy of the Hex Inspector</h2>
  <p>
    The Hex Inspector provides a classic fixed-pitch 3-column layout:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Offset Column:</strong> 8-character hexadecimal byte offset relative to the event start (e.g. <code>00000000</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Hex View (16 Bytes):</strong> Two 8-byte columns formatted as hex byte pairs with color-coding for headers vs. payload.</li>
    <li><strong>ASCII View:</strong> Printable ASCII characters, with unprintable binary control bytes represented by periods (<code>.</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="event-headers">Decoding 19-Byte Binlog Event Headers</h2>
  <p>
    MySQL v4 binlog events start with a standard 19-byte header:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4 not-prose">
    <table class="w-full text-xs font-mono border border-[#2d3b4f] rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#11161d]">
      <thead class="bg-[#161f2c] border-b border-[#2d3b4f] text-[#8b949e]">
        <tr>
          <th class="py-2.5 px-4 text-left">Bytes</th>
          <th class="py-2.5 px-4 text-left">Field</th>
          <th class="py-2.5 px-4 text-left">Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-[#212c3d] text-[#c9d1d9]">
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">0 – 3 (4 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">timestamp</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">Seconds since Unix epoch</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">4 (1 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">type_code</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">Event type identifier (e.g. 0x1e = WRITE_ROWS)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">5 – 8 (4 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">server_id</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">Origin server ID</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">9 – 12 (4 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">event_length</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">Total size of header + payload in bytes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">13 – 16 (4 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">next_position</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">End position offset in the binlog file</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 text-[#38bdf8]">17 – 18 (2 B)</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4 font-semibold">flags</td>
          <td class="py-2 px-4">Event execution flags</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
